\begin_layout Subsection
A Note on the Energy Eigenvalue in the Corrected Schrödinger Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "app:corrected_schrodinger_eigenvalue"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relativistically corrected Schrödinger equation is commonly written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular,
 the physical time dependence of a stationary relativistic state remains 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Psi(\mathbf{r},t)=\psi(\mathbf{r})\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{\mathrm{tot}}t}{\hbar}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

 governs the temporal phase,
 whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{2}_{0}/(2\gamma m_{0})$
\end_inset

 is the eigenvalue of the equivalent stationary Schrödinger-like equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Relativistic Hamiltonian and Scalar Wave Equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "app:relativistic_hamiltonian"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This appendix gives the derivation underlying the two scalar refractive-index models used in Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

.
 A complete relativistic description begins with the Dirac equation.
 When spin and magnetic interactions are not explicitly required,
 elastic electron diffraction can instead be described by the positive-energy,
 spin-independent relativistic Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "fujiwara1961relativistic,kirkland2010advanced"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Minimal Coupling and the Stationary Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time-dependent wave equation is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi(\mathbf{r},t)}{\partial t}=\hat{H}\Psi(\mathbf{r},t),\label{eq:app_time_dependent_wave_equation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with the minimal-coupling Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}=\sqrt{c^{2}\left(\hat{\mathbf{p}}-q_{e}\mathbf{A}/c\right)^{2}+m^{2}_{0}c^{4}}+q_{e}V(\mathbf{r}),\label{eq:app_minimal_coupling_hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{p}}=-i\hbar\nabla$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A}$
\end_inset

 is the magnetic vector potential,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V(\mathbf{r})$
\end_inset

 is the specimen electrostatic potential in volts.
 We use 
\begin_inset Formula $e>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{e}=-e$
\end_inset

,
 so the electron potential energy is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})=q_{e}V(\mathbf{r})=-eV(\mathbf{r}).\label{eq:app_electron_potential_energy}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This notation distinguishes the electrostatic potential 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 from the energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{pot}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neglecting magnetic interactions and introducing the rest energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}=m_{0}c^{2}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}=\sqrt{c^{2}\hat{\mathbf{p}}^{\,2}+m^{2}_{0}c^{4}}+E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})=\sqrt{c^{2}\hat{\mathbf{p}}^{\,2}+E^{2}_{0}}+E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\label{eq:app_relativistic_hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then look for stationnary states 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(\mathbf{r},t)$
\end_inset

 that separate the temporal and spatial contributions :
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Psi(\mathbf{r},t)=\psi(\mathbf{r})\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{\mathrm{tot}}t}{\hbar}\right),\label{eq:app_stationary_ansatz}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The stationary equation is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\psi=E_{\mathrm{tot}}\psi.\label{eq:app_stationary_equation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

 is the conserved total relativistic energy,
 including the rest energy.
 As we look for elastic interaction this energy is conserved.
 In particular,
 in vacuum,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{tot}}=\gamma E_{0}=\gamma m_{0}c^{2}=\sqrt{c^{2}p^{2}_{0}+E^{2}_{0}},\qquad p_{0}=hk_{0}=\frac{h}{\lambda}=\gamma m_{0}v_{0},\label{eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equations on the left allow to get the interesting equation :
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{2}_{o}=(\gamma^{2}-1)m^{2}_{0}c^{2}=\gamma^{2}_{0}m^{2}_{0}v^{2}_{0}$
\end_inset

 that link the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}$
\end_inset

et 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{0}=v_{0}/c$
\end_inset

 ;
 one gets by dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{2}_{0}c^{2}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma^{2}_{0}(1-\beta^{2}_{0})=1$
\end_inset

 i.e.
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}$
\end_inset

is positive :
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}_{0}}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 can also be used to link the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}$
\end_inset

et 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{0}=v_{0}/c$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 being respectively the speeds of the electron and light in vacuum;
 by taking the square of this equation and using the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=\gamma m_{0}v_{0}$
\end_inset

) one obtains :
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma^{_{2}}_{0}m^{2}_{0}c^{4}=\gamma^{_{2}}_{0}m^{2}_{0}v^{2}_{0}c^{4}+m_{0}c^{4}$
\end_inset

;
 and dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{2}_{0}c^{4}$
\end_inset

it gives :
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma^{2}_{0}(1-\beta^{2}_{0})=1$
\end_inset

 i.e.
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}$
\end_inset

is positive :
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}_{0}}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the beam has been accelerated through a voltage magnitude 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{\mathrm{acc}}>0$
\end_inset

,
 the total energy is the sum of the rest and kinetic energies :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}=eU_{\mathrm{acc}}=E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{0}=(\gamma_{0}-1)E_{0}$
\end_inset

 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}=(E_{kin}+E_{0})/E_{0}=1+E_{kin}/E_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 It also interesting the introduce the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=v_{0}/c$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 being respectively the speeds of the electron and light in vacuum,.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By definition 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=m_{0}\gamma v_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 can be used to compute the wave length 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}$
\end_inset

of the electron in vaccuum.:
 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{2}p^{2}_{0}=h^{2}c^{2}/\lambda^{2}_{0}=E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-E^{2}_{0}=(E_{\mathrm{kin}}+E_{0})^{2}-E^{2}_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}=hc/\sqrt{E^{2}_{kin}+2E_{kin}E_{0}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Klein–Gordon and Corrected Schrödinger Forms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rearranging Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_stationary_equation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and squaring gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[c^{2}\hat{\mathbf{p}}^{\,2}+E^{2}_{0}\right]\psi=\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]^{2}\psi.\label{eq:app_kg_before_linearisation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A local relativistic momentum and a local wavelength can be defined :
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c^{2}\hat{\mathbf{p}}^{\,2}=\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]^{2}-E^{2}_{0}=h^{2}c^{2}\mathbf{k}^{2}\label{eq:app_kg_before_linearisation2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{p}}^{\,2}=-\hbar^{2}\nabla^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=hk_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 equation 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla^{2}\psi(\mathbf{r})+(2\pi k_{0})^{2}n^{2}_{\mathrm{KG}}(\mathbf{r})\psi(\mathbf{r})=0,\label{eq:app_kg_helmholtz_form}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
n^{2}_{\mathrm{KG}}(\mathbf{r})=\frac{\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]^{2}-E^{2}_{0}}{E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-E^{2}_{0}}=\frac{p(\mathbf{r})}{p_{0}}=\frac{k(\mathbf{r})}{k_{0}}.\label{eq:app_kg_refractive_index}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\mathrm{KG}}$
\end_inset

 is the ratio of the local relativistic momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p(r)$
\end_inset

 to the vacuum momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}$
\end_inset

 .
 This connects the Klein–Gordon form to the electron-optical refractive-index concepts of Glaser and Ehrenberg–Siday 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Glaser1933,Ehrenberg-Siday1949"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $|E_{\mathrm{pot}}|\ll E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

,
 as it is the case for electron-beam interaction,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]^{2}\simeq E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-2E_{\mathrm{tot}}E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r}).\label{eq:app_linearised_energy}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}/c^{2}=\gamma m_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_kg_before_linearisation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 then reduces to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla^{2}\psi(\mathbf{r})+\left[(2\pi k_{0})^{2}-\frac{2\gamma m_{0}}{\hbar^{2}}E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]\psi(\mathbf{r})=0.\label{eq:app_corrected_schrodinger}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or equivalently ,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2\gamma m_{0}}\nabla^{2}+E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf{r})\right]\psi(\mathbf{r})=\frac{p^{2}_{0}}{2\gamma m_{0}}\psi(\mathbf{r}).\label{eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is therefore a stationary Schrödinger-like reformulation of the relativistic wave equation,
 rather than the original relativistic energy-eigenvalue equation.
 This is the relativistically corrected Schrödinger form as deduced by Fujiwara.
 The right-hand side has the algebraic fomm of the nonrelativistic kinetic energy expression 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{2}_{0}/(2m_{0})$
\end_inset

,
 with however the rest mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{0}$
\end_inset

 replaced by the relativistic mass 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma m_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 It should not,
 however,
 be identified with either the total relativistic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

 or,
 in general,
 the kinetic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}=E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Indeed,
 using 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\gamma m_{0}=\frac{E_{\mathrm{tot}}}{c^{2}},\qquad p^{2}_{0}c^{2}=E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-E^{2}_{0},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the eigenvalue in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{p^{2}_{0}}{2\gamma m_{0}}=\frac{E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-E^{2}_{0}}{2E_{\mathrm{tot}}}=E_{\mathrm{kin}}\frac{E_{\mathrm{tot}}+E_{0}}{2E_{\mathrm{tot}}}=E_{\mathrm{kin}}\frac{\gamma+1}{2\gamma}.\label{eq:app_effective_schrodinger_eigenvalue}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It approaches 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}$
\end_inset

 only in the nonrelativistic limit 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}\ll E_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}\simeq E_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation xxxx-mettrelabonneREF indicates that the energy appearing in the temporal dependence of the wave function is not 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0\mathrm{kin}}$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

.
 The factor 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

 arises when the scalar relativistic wave equation is linearised in the specimen potential and divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $2E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interaction Constant and Paraxial Limit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The conventional TEM interaction constant is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma=\frac{2\pi\gamma m_{0}e}{h^{2}k_{0}}=\frac{2\pi\gamma m_{0}e\lambda}{h^{2}}=2\pi ek_{0}\frac{\gamma m_{0}c^{2}}{h^{2}k^{2}_{0}c^{2}}=\frac{2\pi eE_{\mathrm{tot}}}{hc\sqrt{E^{2}_{\mathrm{tot}}-E^{2}_{0}}}.\label{eq:app_sigma_definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential term in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_corrected_schrodinger"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 can therefore be written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{2\gamma m_{0}e}{\hbar^{2}}V(\mathbf{r})=4\pi k_{0}\sigma V(\mathbf{r}),\label{eq:app_potential_term_sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

giving 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla^{2}\psi(\mathbf{r})+\left[(2\pi k_{0})^{2}+4\pi k_{0}\sigma V(\mathbf{r})\right]\psi(\mathbf{r})=0.\label{eq:app_corrected_schrodinger_sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Factoring out the carrier wave,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf{r})=\exp(2\pi ik_{0}z)\tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{r}),\label{eq:app_carrier_wave}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and neglecting 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial^{2}\tilde{\psi}/\partial z^{2}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\tilde{\psi}}{\partial z}=\frac{i}{4\pi k_{0}}\nabla^{2}_{\perp}\tilde{\psi}+i\sigma V(\mathbf{r})\tilde{\psi}.\label{eq:app_paraxial_sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For a slice with projected electrostatic potential 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{j}(x,y)=\int^{z_{j}+\Delta z}_{z_{j}}V(x,y,z)\,dz,\label{eq:app_projected_potential}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the phase-grating transmission function is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{j}(x,y)=\exp\left[i\sigma V_{j}(x,y)\right],\label{eq:app_transmission_function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the expression used in the main text.
\end_layout

